fordable compared to AoNZ. Streets are bustling with people, cars and mopeds (although nothing like Vietnam!) Air quality is ok but lots of petrol fumes near busy roads. No jaywalking and everyone waits patiently for the pedestrian crossing light to turn green. The language barrier is a challenge even with AI to help out — most of the time I’m pretty much the only Westerner wandering around the city - luckily people speak some English but I really need to invest time in getting skilled up with more than just “Xiexie” (thank you)… Mobile / internet connectivity everywhere I’ve been has been super reliable (roaming on 2Degrees SIM, just NZ$8/day all in - I could get an eSIM for less, no doubt, if I was here longer) The landmark Taipei 101 is *really tall*… (up in the clouds the day I visited!) The day starts late and goes on long into the night (night markets are plentiful throughout Taipei)… as a result my 7am cafe rhythm has struggled, particularly adjusting from AoNZ timezone. The urban soundscape is",
